  "verbalization": "Fumiquinazoline B is shown to have a target relationship with CD276 antigen through multiple pathways, with the strongest evidence linking it to the CD276 antigen via the Thioredoxin, mitochondrial pathway. This pathway is supported by a final score of 0.5348, indicating a plausible biological connection. The drug is also associated with Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, which is further targeted by Flavipin and (Salicylato-O1,O2)silver, both of which have a target relationship with CD276 antigen. While the direct mechanism linking Fumiquinazoline B to CD276 antigen remains unclear, the evidence suggests a potential role in modulating the thioredoxin/mitochondrial pathway, which may influence CD276 expression or function. The presence of multiple targets in this pathway, including Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, and their downstream targets, supports a coordinated regulatory mechanism that could contribute to the observed effect on CD276 antigen. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the biological network involved, and further investigation is warranted to elucidate the precise mechanism by which Fumiquinazoline B interacts with CD276 antigen.",
